mes saw many civilians killed  their first visit to ukraine since  russia invaded was a chance to show  europe's support  and counter criticism over the speed and  scale of their  europe is by responses side  it will remain so for as long as  necessary until victory is achieved  in the form of a return to peace in a  free and independent ukraine  the opposite of the consequences of the  barbarism that we have seen together  this morning in urpin  in a joint press conference in kiev  ukrainian president vladimir zelinsky  called for more heavy weaponry  positioning his country as europe's  eastern flank  russia does not choose whom and when to  threaten  whom in europe to grant safety  russian aggression against ukraine is  aggression against all of europe  against the united europe  against every one of us  against our values  must be united  the european leaders pledged more  military aid and backed immediate  candidate status for ukraine joining the  european union  ukraine belongs within the european  family  and the milestone on its journey toward  the eu would be to receive candidate  status  the eu is set to discuss ukraine's  candidacy on friday but even if all  member states agree it could still take  years for keeve to gain actual  membership  so roman a show of solidarity there for  ukraine by the leaders of the four eu  countries uh was that as much as  vladimir zelinski could hope for or was  he expecting more  well the landscape himself said he was  satisfied i think he was pretty happy  with that support for the ukrainian eu  bid  uh maybe he was a bit disappointed that  there were no uh or very few new  commitments to to supply ukraine with  arms with heavy arms uh with artillery  in the first place  and i think he might have been  disappointed that there was um there  were no specific commitments uh as to  the sanctions new sanctions against  russia especially no new sanctions um  no embargo on russian gas which is which  would hit russia really hard and this is  something that ukraine is hoping for uh  but in general i think ukraine uh is  rather satisfied with the results of  that visit yes  if ukraine does formally become an eu  candidate country roman would that  change anything in the effort to end the  russian invasion  well this will not change  anything immediately on the battlefield  but we shouldn't underestimate it's an  extremely important  sign a sign of political support for  ukraine  it's a moral booster for the ukrainian  society which  has been hoping for that  chance to be to become an eu member for  for many decades for two decades  actually and um it's it's a historic  event and we've heard those words  historic yesterday during the press  conference several times but practically  speaking uh this will give ukraine more  money from the european union to conduct  reforms  to  to strengthen democracy and it will give  also  new expertise to ukraine will give the  european union a possibility to send  more experts to ukraine to help it  become a member of the european union  one day  roman thank you very much that was dw's  roman goncharenko in kiev  so today the european commission will  issue its recommendation on ukraine's  application for eu membership but the  final decision on that status rests with  the leaders of the eu member states  themselves who meet for a council summit  next week the commission is expected to  recommend candidacy status for ukraine  and neighboring moldova  but there's a host of other states  who've been waiting for years to join  the eu here in dark blue notably turkey  and countries in the western balkans  well for more let's cross over to our  brussels bureau chief alexandre from  alexander tell us more about the  recommendation that the commission is  expected to issue today  well indeed terry we expect the european  commission to recommend that ukraine and  by the way moldova as well be granted  candidate status however it will be  interesting to see how they are going to  express  this opinion because we know that the  decision about that was postponed a  couple of times because of course it's  not an easy decision and some officials  are saying that it's  ukraine has a lot  to do still they have to fight  corruption they have to do more to  improve the rule of law situation in the  country and nevertheless we expect the  european commission to make this  recommendation today especially of  course now after france italy and  germany threw their weight behind this  idea however when it happens today it  could be that this decision will be  connected  with conditions and  of course it will be only the beginning  of a very long process let's have a look  at our explainer  ukraine may be on its way into the  european union  and some leaders have promised it won't  take long  it will be  not as usual a matter of years to form  this opinion but i think a matter of  weeks if we work closely together  so for you  thank you  but despite the public show of  solidarity the eu commission president  displayed here on her first visit to  ukraine there are a few legal challenges  ahead  candidacy is the first step countries  have to fulfill the so-called copenhagen  criteria which includes having a stable  democratic government  that applies the rule of law  the second stage are accession talks  which mark the beginning of practical  preparations for eu membership so a  country would usually need to undergo a  reform process which includes its  judiciary the administration its economy  and political structures  the rules add up to 35 chapters in total  with long lists of conditions countries  are vetted accordingly by the commission  on the state of their markets  agriculture external relations and other  factors  if eu negotiators find a problem like  government meddling in the appointment  of judges they can demand justice  reforms which can take years  finland needed three years to comply  turkey started the negotiating phase  back in 2005 but talks have long been  deadlocked due to ankara's increasingly  authoritarian tendencies and the  leadership's unwillingness to reform and  in the case of ukraine the french  president seems to have low expectations  we all know perfectly well that the  process allowing them to join will take  several years in truth probably several  decades  [Music]  [Applause]  once all 35 chapters of requirements are  satisfied and all member states have  signed off on them together with the  european commission and the european  parliament an accession treaty is put  forward it is then signed and ratified  and voila you are a eu member states  in the mid 2000s 12 mostly eastern  european countries were allowed to join  the bloc  despite apparent issues with  dysfunctional institutions and  deep-seated corruption  the understanding was that for former  communist countries change was more  difficult to bring about  a political decision was made  ukraine today has institutional issues  as well  on top of that it will need massive  reconstruction efforts after the war  and it seems that at the moment not  everyone in the eu is motivated to take  them in  so the path to eu membership can be long  and difficult  and some members do oppose membership  for ukraine some of the eu member states  now alexander tell us more about that  yeah we're talking about states uh such  as portugal for instance or denmark or  the never lands they are saying that  it's not the right thing to to to grant  ukraine a  status because the country is at war any  accession talks could only begin after  the war is over they are also saying  that it would be not fair to  for ukraine to jump the queue  when we consider that are that they are  there are other states such as  montenegro north macedonia for instance  who have been in the waiting room for  years even though they are official  candidate states and there are also  voices saying that  the european union itself is not ready  to welcome new 